A||||5||Thor||Allignment Scam?||||1||
Z||000000||Thor||04-23-2001||11:30 PM||||Why?...All I wanted was a rear allignment after I installed new struts and shocks.  my local Champion auto store offered a full wheel allignment for 55 bucks.  OK. I bring the 86GT five speed in this morning feeling pretty good about myself because the drive from my home to the shop was very incouraging.  A very slight wobble at very slow speeds. No dogtracking and instead of the car going Bang-Bang-Bang over rough road it went bib-bib-bib. I told the technician that the struts were aftermarket with a slotted lower mounting hole so there would be no need for a camber kit.  Fine. After a bit I see the tech' on the phone with a Pontiac dealer ordering A passinger side inner tie rod for 102 bucks...I interupted.  His explination was I needed this tie rod because It's a little loose and they can't do the job untill it's replaced...That may be true but the guy did'nt even tell me about this problem before picking up the phone.  Or alert me that replacing the inner tie rod was going to be an additional 200 dollors. Of course one of the reasons for that price was due to the fact that he was going to get the part from A DEALER! I got the beautiful-why would anybody want to modify car back[after spending the 25 dollor inspection fee] and checked the manual. I could see that the task of replacing this part was indeed going to be a b****. Yet the car seems to be handeling quite nicelly. In conclusion, what I would really like to do is wait till I put the car in storage this fall and dismantle,inspect and replace any needed front end parts. In the interum: Is it possible to have just the rear wheels alligned? Or is the relationship of front and rear wheels so critical that this can't be achieved? For heavens sake I was able to replace the shocks and struts, rotors and rotor/hubs, front bearings and an amp/subwoofer for under 500 bucks. Now, the money I was going to spend on a good CD reciever would have to go into a new tie rod and wheel allignment...There must be a better way! <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||04-24-2001||12:12 AM||||All 4 wheels need to be aligned together in order to do it correctly. If there is something loose or bad, it can cause all kinds of handling problems. It is better to get it fixed right away. Why did he call a dealer? NAPA, AutoZone, Pep Boys, Trak, etc all carry those in stock for a lot less than the dealer. I personally would have driven home and checked out/replaced the parts myself and then drove back for the alignment.<BR>(Sorry, I don't trust too many people to work on my cars)||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||04-24-2001||12:31 AM||||Well the could have done it for you anyway. I had a shop do an alignment for me and they were happy to do it. Well about 4 months later my left front wheel hub broke (they told me it wouldnt last long). So the upshot is I had to get it realigned after the hub was put on. Another 35 bucks.||||reg||3
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||04-24-2001||01:55 AM||||Replacing an inner tie rod is no big deal.<BR>I think you have an Auto Zone near by that has a tool rental program. Get the proper tool(s) for your job. You need pickle forks (ball joint/tie rod end seperator) the inner tie rod end tool and the socket that fits the inner tie rod end.<BR>Follow the instructions in the book and you should have no problems.||||reg||1
Z||000004||pontiacs4me||04-24-2001||03:16 AM||||I took my firebird to firestone about a year ago to have it aligned.  I had just bought brand new 235/60/15 tires for the front, and didnt want them to get torn up.  They called me later that afternoon with the good news.  It was going to cost me 750 bucks, because the ball joints were worn out, and I needed some kind of camber correction kit, and all this crap I have never heard of.  I know alot about cars and they were naming stuff that Herb Adams has probably never heard of.  So I found the phone number to a guy that owns a suspension performance shop here in wichita, (I met him when I worked at a hot rod shop about a year and a half ago), I told him what firestone told me, and he laughed.  He told me he would rebuild my entire front suspension with poly-graphite bushings, and an alignment for 500 bucks.  I went back and picked up my bird, and they charged me 25 bucks to put it on a rack and look under my car.  Ive been driving on those tires for about 12,000 miles now, and they have been wearing perfectly, the car handles fine, and I havent replaced anything but the endlinks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||04-24-2001||10:26 PM||||I also had a run in getting an alignment...<BR>The clown loosened my front upper control arm bolt...<BR>Said I needed a complete front axle assembly...<BR>I took it home tightened the bolt to specs and took it to another shop that had no problem aligning it...||||reg||1
